Rebuild the kernel when our kernel-side patches change.

common_drivers is compiled into the kernel image, not against it. Its own
package stamp covers its patches, so the driver rebuilds - but the kernel
does not, and the image keeps the previously built driver. Naming our patch
directories in LINUX_DEPENDS puts them in the linux package's
PKG_NEED_UNPACK, and in every kernel package's, so a change invalidates all
of them together.

diff --git a/config/options b/config/options
--- a/config/options
+++ b/config/options
@@ -66,12 +66,16 @@
 fi
 
 # projects can set KERNEL_NAME (kernel.img)
 KERNEL_NAME="${KERNEL_NAME:-KERNEL}"
 
 LINUX_DEPENDS="${PROJECT_DIR}/${PROJECT}/linux ${PROJECT_DIR}/${PROJECT}/patches/linux ${PROJECT_DIR}/${PROJECT}/packages/linux ${ROOT}/packages/linux"
+# Our patches to anything compiled into the kernel. common_drivers is built as
+# part of the kernel image rather than against it, so its own package stamp
+# rebuilding is not enough - the kernel has to rebuild with it.
+LINUX_DEPENDS+=" ${PROJECT_DIR}/${PROJECT}/patches-yacer/linux ${PROJECT_DIR}/${PROJECT}/patches-yacer/common_drivers"
 [ -n "${DEVICE}" ] && LINUX_DEPENDS+=" ${PROJECT_DIR}/${PROJECT}/devices/${DEVICE}/linux ${PROJECT_DIR}/${PROJECT}/devices/${DEVICE}/patches/linux ${PROJECT_DIR}/${PROJECT}/devices/${DEVICE}/packages/linux"
 
 # Need to point to your actual cc
 # If you have ccache installed, take care that LOCAL_CC does not point to it
 [ -z "${LOCAL_CC}" ] && export LOCAL_CC="$(command -v gcc)"
 
